    "lawCode": "Amd §§201-a, 202, 202-a, 202-b & 202-bb, St Ad Proc Act",
    "lawSection": "State Administrative Procedure Act",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7097\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the state administrative procedure\nact, in relation to online posting of full text of rules, statements\nand analyses\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill amends the State Administrative Procedure Act (SAPA) to\nrequire that when only a summary of the text of a proposed or revised\nrule or another regulatory document published in the State Register,\nthe full text must be made available to the public by posting on a\nstate agency website.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nBill § -1 amends SAPA § 201-a to require that when a job impact\nstatement is summarized for publication in the State Register because\nit exceeds 2000 words, the full text shall be posted on the website of\nthe agency (or of another state entity).\n\nBill § § 2 and 3 amend SAPA § 202(1) to establish similar requirements\nfor online posting of the full text of a proposed rule over 2000 words\nfor which only a summary is published in the State Register.  The full\ntext shall remain posted until the proposed rule is adopted, revised,\nwithdrawn or expires pursuant to SAPA Article 2. Such web posting is\nnot required for the full text of a \"rate-type\" rule as defined in\nSAPA § io2(2)(a)(ii). Similar requirements are added requiring web\nposting of summarized regulatory impact statements and flexibility\nanalyses. Finally, current language requiring extension of the comment\nperiod when the full text of a summarized rule is not posted is\ndeleted since it would no longer be applicable.\n\nBill § 4 amends SAPA § 202(4-a) to establish requirements similar to\nthose prescribed for proposed rules for online posting of the full\ntext of a revised rule over 2000 words.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nWhen the text of a proposed rule or other rulemaking document exceeds\n2000 words, only a summary is published in the State Register. In\norder to review the actual text, a member of the public must contact\nthe agency and ask that they send the full text. This shortens the\ntime available for the public to review and comment on a lengthy\nproposal. As a result, SAPA requires that if a proposed rule is\nsummarized in the State Register and the full text is not posted on a\nstate website, the comment period is expended from 45 days to 60 days.\n\nThis requirement, however, only applies to the original proposal. No\nweb posting is currently required for a revised rule - even if the\ntext has been extensively revised - or for regulatory impact\nstatements, job impact statements or flexibility analyses for small\nbusinesses, local governments or rural areas. Given the growing\nimportance of electronic media, there is no reason that rulemaking\ninformation should not be readily available online. Since the State\nRegister is already available online, requiring relevant documents\nthat are only summarized in that publication to also be posted online\nwill make the regulatory process in New York more transparent,\neffective and timely.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe first day of January after having become a law, and applicable to\nany notice of proposed or revised rule making (including rule text and\nother regulatory documents) published on or after such date.\n",
